WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.361 --> 00:00:07.853
♪ Smoking cigarettes and
watching "Captain Kangaroo" ♪

00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:19.622
♪ Tell me I've nothing to do ♪

00:00:23.768 --> 00:00:28.746
♪ It's good to see you I must
go I know I look a fright ♪

00:00:28.746 --> 00:00:29.579
- Mother f-

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.730
Narrator: You don't
have to be a movie buff.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.180
Maybe you don't even
like movies that much.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.740
But everyone has heard of
the name Quentin Tarantino.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:54.650
He is, without a doubt,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.360
one of the most celebrated
directors of our time,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.610
with each of his eight iconic works

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.340
making a profound impact
on the history of cinema

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.560
and inspiring generations of filmmakers

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.380
with a vastly new style
and approach to filmmaking

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.920
that can only be described
as Tarantino-esque,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.300
which, by the way, is
now an official entry

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.050
in the Oxford English Dictionary.

00:01:19.044 --> 00:01:22.850
So, what makes the films of
Quentin Tarantino so special?

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.310
Many often cite his razor-sharp dialogue.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.883
- You gonna bark all day, little doggy?

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:30.480
Or are you gonna bite?

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.620
Narrator: The often graphic,
yet stylized, violence.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.800
Or his use of nonlinear
narrative structure.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.550
But what truly sets him apart
from every other filmmaker

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.350
is the way in which he
steals from other movies.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.260
And that's not just a figure of speech.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.550
In a 1994 interview with Empire magazine,

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:55.330
Tarantino said, "I steal from
every single movie ever made."

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.920
Tarantino's visual references to movies

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.040
have become his trademark.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.570
Some of these references
are merely hinted at.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.674
- The D is silent.

00:02:04.674 --> 00:02:05.840
- I know.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.063
Narrator: While others are
almost identical replications.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.790
For this reason,

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:13.790
he's been the center of
controversy for many years.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.810
For example, in 1997, his
debut film, "Reservoir Dogs,"

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.470
was under heavy scrutiny after a critic

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:22.686
accused Tarantino of plagiarizing

00:02:22.686 --> 00:02:27.100
the 1987 Hong Kong crime
film "City on Fire."

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.460
The final 20 minutes of "City on Fire"

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.490
is essentially identical to
the plot of "Reservoir Dogs,"

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.090
and there are shots and
moments scattered throughout

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.923
that directly resemble each other.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.203
Including this famous
Mexican-standoff sequence.

00:02:44.213 --> 00:02:45.046
(shouting in a foreign language)

00:02:45.046 --> 00:02:47.220
But it's not just in this film.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.450
Almost all of Tarantino's eight films

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.170
have a main source of inspiration.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.300
For "Jackie Brown," it was
the 1974 film "Foxy Brown."

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.680
For "Kill Bill," the 1973
Japanese film "Lady Snowblood."

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.220
And his "Inglourious Basterds"
is, in a lot of ways,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:07.810
similar to the 1967 war
film "The Dirty Dozen."

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:11.050
And on top of that, each film
has more visual references

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.810
to at least another dozen movies.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.570
Many consider these similarities homages,

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:19.970
a practice as long as the
history of cinema itself,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.980
a way for Tarantino to pay respect

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.463
to the movies he loves.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.060
But Tarantino explicitly denies this.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.227
In the same interview, he goes on to say,

00:03:29.227 --> 00:03:31.157
"Great artists steal.

00:03:31.157 --> 00:03:33.440
They don't do homages."

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.120
It's a quote that closely
resembles words attributed

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.420
to another famous artist: Pablo Picasso,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.061
who's often quoted as having said,

00:03:41.061 --> 00:03:42.802
"Good artists copy,

00:03:42.802 --> 00:03:45.030
and great artists steal."

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:48.610
To understand why and
how Tarantino steals,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:51.530
it's important to
understand his background.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.740
Tarantino's career in film
didn't start in a classroom

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.860
or even a movie set, but a video store,

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:00.350
where he worked as a clerk
and gained a reputation

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:04.090
for his almost encyclopedic
knowledge of cinema.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:04.923
In other words,

00:04:04.923 --> 00:04:07.900
Tarantino was never
taught how to make a film.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.630
Instead, he learned how to
make films by watching them,

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.780
which makes it natural that imitation

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:17.320
became his main source
of inspiration and style.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.270
In fact, if you take a look

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.160
at most of Tarantino's screenplays,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.080
they begin with a list of filmmakers

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.183
whom the stories were
inspired by and dedicated to.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.520
I think the reason why
Tarantino is so proud

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.280
to admit that he is stealing

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.300
is because he accomplishes
something with it

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.380
that no other filmmaker
is quite capable of:

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.530
creating something new.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.420
And as paradoxical as it may sound,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.270
Tarantino's movies have a
sense of originality to them,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.820
despite their many sources of inspiration.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:47.870
This is why Tarantino is often hailed

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.650
as one of the quintessential
filmmakers of postmodernism.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.210
Postmodernism in film describes an era

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.230
when filmmakers began questioning

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.940
the ways in which mainstream
movies are made and told

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.030
and began making movies that
went directly against it.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.320
One of the central tenets of postmodernism

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.740
is the idea that nothing is new in art;

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:14.130
everything is recycled and
reused over and over again.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.800
"Reservoir Dogs" might have
stolen the Mexican standoff

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.370
from "City on Fire," but
"City on Fire" also stole it

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.600
from the 1966 film "The
Good, the Bad and the Ugly."

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.640
And "Pulp Fiction" is no exception;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.940
it's chock-full of references
to classical movies,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.910
especially movies from the
French New Wave movement,

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.660
one of the most influential movements

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.230
in the history of cinema,

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:39.050
in which young filmmakers
also tried to challenge

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:41.430
the traditional method of filmmaking.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.970
Its famous dancing sequence
was inspired by this sequence

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.310
from the 1964 film "Band of Outsiders."

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.960
And the choreography
closely resembles this scene

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.200
from the 1963 film "8 1/2."

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.920
But that's not the only
source it steals from.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.750
John Travolta's dance was inspired

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:03.000
by this scene from 1966
adaptation of "Batman,"

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.260
while Uma Thurman's dancing resembles that

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:09.430
of a cat in the 1970 animated
film "The Aristocats."

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.610
And throughout the rest
of the film as well.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.220
The mysterious suitcase that
carries the plot of the film

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.178
is a replication from
the 1955 American film

00:06:18.178 --> 00:06:19.686
"Kiss Me Deadly."

00:06:19.686 --> 00:06:21.790
And, of course, this scene was inspired

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:23.543
by Alfred Hitchcock's "Psycho."

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.310
What makes Tarantino so special

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:29.560
is that he never steals from one source.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:33.517
He rather steals from multiple
sources spanning decades

00:06:33.517 --> 00:06:37.270
and then stitches them together
to create something new.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:39.210
It's a technique known as pastiche,

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.020
a vital element in postmodernism.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.310
Most people are more
familiar with this technique

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.710
through another medium: music,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.670
and especially in the hip-hop genre,

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.250
where artists use sampling

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.030
to take part of an existing song

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:53.877
to create something new.

00:06:53.877 --> 00:06:56.403
♪ You said you'd care for care for me ♪

00:06:56.403 --> 00:06:57.679
♪ Said you'd be there for me ♪

00:06:57.679 --> 00:06:58.512
♪ Care for me ♪

00:06:58.512 --> 00:06:59.823
♪ I know you care for me ♪

00:06:59.823 --> 00:07:01.295
♪ There for me there for me ♪

00:07:01.295 --> 00:07:02.128
♪ Said you'd be there for me ♪

00:07:02.128 --> 00:07:03.720
And just like Tarantino,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.580
it's been the subject of
controversy many times.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.950
Tarantino's pastiche works so
well because of two reasons.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:15.910
One is his understanding of
the subject he's stealing from.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:18.210
More often than not, homages in movies

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.290
are a shallow and vain attempt

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.660
at imitating an iconic moment,

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.240
and they rarely serve a purpose.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.270
But Tarantino's references
are often seamless

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:31.510
and easy to miss because
they enhance the scenes

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:34.100
and the genre he experiments with.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.480
And if you take a look
at Tarantino's career,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.780
each of his eight films is a tribute

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:42.060
to a specific genre
and movement in cinema.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.160
"Reservoir Dogs" is a pastiche

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.320
of the gritty Hong Kong crime
films, and "Pulp Fiction"

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:50.910
is based on the unconventional
French New Wave movement.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.530
"Jackie Brown" basis itself

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.880
off the '70s' controversial
blaxploitation films,

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.710
while "Kill Bill" is
reminiscent of the classical

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.277
Japanese samurai and
Chinese kung fu movies.

00:08:02.277 --> 00:08:04.552
"Death Proof" pays tribute to low-budget

00:08:04.552 --> 00:08:05.701
exploitation movies,

00:08:05.701 --> 00:08:10.100
while "Inglourious Basterds"
references World War II cinema.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.540
And his two most recent
films, "Django Unchained"

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.750
and "The Hateful Eight," are modern takes

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.023
of the Italian spaghetti Westerns.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.790
Tarantino seamlessly
blends all these genres

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:24.780
and inspirations through his
unique vision and writing.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.570
This is where his razor-sharp
dialogue comes in.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:28.403
- Gentlemen.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:30.623
You had my curiosity,

00:08:31.589 --> 00:08:33.350
but now you have my attention.

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.070
Narrator: It's not an exaggeration to say

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.610
that Tarantino's films are essentially

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:41.200
readaptations of
classical films and genres

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.920
that take place in a world of Tarantino,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.520
where violence, injustice, sex,

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.540
and satirical cynicism flourish.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.870
Quentin Tarantino perhaps
knows better than anyone

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.820
that you don't have to
look far for inspiration.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:57.500
Most of the time, it might be somewhere

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.530
close and familiar to us.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.170
My guess is that, for Tarantino,

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.670
it's in the aisles of VHS tapes

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.320
that he grew up watching as a child.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.350
Tarantino proudly plays the role

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:10.990
of a masterful thief of cinema.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.510
And as long as he continues

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.510
to make a masterpiece out of them,

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.191
it's the kind of thievery I'd
be more than happy to accept.

00:09:18.191 --> 00:09:21.109
- You know something, Utivich?

00:09:21.109 --> 00:09:24.609
I think this just might be my masterpiece.

